  "verbalization": "Cefaclor, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is linked to Urinary Tract Infection (UTI) through a chain of indications that involve pneumonia and other bacterial pathogens. The evidence suggests that Cefaclor is used to treat pneumonia, which in turn is indicated for conditions like Cefmetazole and Cefamandole, both of which are also used to treat UTIs. This implies a potential pathway where Cefaclor, through its use in treating pneumonia, may indirectly contribute to the treatment of UTIs, though the direct causal link is not explicitly stated. The presence of multiple indirect pathways supports the idea that Cefaclor could be repurposed for UTI, but the exact mechanism of action remains unclear. The integration of these paths highlights the importance of considering broader clinical indications when evaluating drug repurposing.",
